Correction: the output above is incomplete and not in the required format. Here is the full transcription.

<page type="text">
  <layout>One column of verse, full width, under the running head; a rule and a fleuron ornament below the text. No margin notes.</layout>
  <running_head>ALIUD EJUSDEM.</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <p>Sicut apis, saltus, hortosq; &amp; olentia nacta<br/>Pascua, per flores &amp; thyma summa volat:</p>
    <p>Actore hinc carpit, summo tenus optima quaq;<br/>Et dulcis fingit nobile mellis opus:</p>
    <p>Sic per Musarum, Tempeq; &amp; amoena vireta<br/>Bilstenius volitans, optima quaq; legit.</p>
    <p>Atq; hinc gignit opus, genitumq; exponit in auras,<br/>Forsitan hoc Solis lucida tela fugit?</p>
    <p>Nil minus: ingenuanam totum continet orbem<br/>Doctrina, &amp; multis eruta sensa libris.</p>
    <p>Non in aperto igitur passim proflare veretur,<br/>Serviat ut studiis, Grex studiosa, tuis.</p>
    <p>Hoc lege, sed lectum, bene percipe, pectore conde:<br/>Hoc exprome usu, cum feret usus, opus.</p>
    <p>Nec vero hoc satis est: plus ultra, Symbolon esto<br/>(Dum contacta tibi ex ultima meta) tuum.</p>
    <p>Post ea quae Sophia Proceres monumenta dedere,<br/>Illa leges oculis, illa teres manibus.</p>
    <p>Virtutis sectare viam: comitabitur illam,<br/>Ingrediare, labor: progrediaris, amor.</p>
    <p>Emensio continget honor, pulcerrima merces<br/>Ergo age, virtutis perfice gnauus iter.</p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Verse is printed in italic throughout; the lines are set as paragraph stanzas with line breaks. Doubtful readings: "summo tenus" (printed "summotenus") and "ingenua nam" (printed "ingenuanam") are read as printed with the words run together. "Emensio" is read as "Emensio"; the scan's "Emenso" may be a misreading. Fleuron below the text is an ornament, not transcribed.</notes>
</page>